      "text": "To solve the equation \\( f^{-1}(x) = f((2x)^{-1}) \\) for the function \\( f(x) = 16x + 3 \\), we first need to find the inverse function \\( f^{-1}(x) \\).\n\nThe function \\( f(x) = 16x + 3 \\) can be expressed in terms of \\( y \\) as follows:\n\\[ y = 16x + 3. \\]\nTo find the inverse function, we solve for \\( x \\) in terms of \\( y \\):\n\\[ y - 3 = 16x, \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{y - 3}{16}. \\]\nThus, the inverse function is\n\\[ f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{x - 3}{16}. \\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\( f((2x)^{-1}) \\) into the equation. First, we find \\( (2x)^{-1} \\):\n\\[ (2x)^{-1} = \\frac{1}{2x}. \\]\nSo we have\n\\[ f\\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) = 16 \\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) + 3 = \\frac{16}{2x} + 3 = \\frac{8}{x} + 3. \\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\( \\frac{8}{x} + 3 \\) into \\( f^{-1}(x) \\):\n\\[ f^{-1}\\left( \\frac{8}{x} + 3 \\right) = \\frac{\\left( \\frac{8}{x} + 3 \\right) - 3}{16} = \\frac{\\frac{8}{x}}{16} = \\frac{8}{x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{16} = \\frac{8}{16x} = \\frac{1}{2x}. \\]\n\nWe are given that \\( f^{-1}(x) = f((2x)^{-1}) \\), so we have:\n\\[ \\frac{x - 3}{16} = \\frac{1}{2x}. \\]\nTo clear the fractions, we multiply both sides by \\( 16x \\):\n\\[ x(x - 3) = 8. \\]\nThis simplifies to the quadratic equation:\n\\[ x^2 - 3x - 8 = 0. \\]\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -3 \\), and \\( c = -8 \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-8)}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 + 32}}{2} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{41}}{2}. \\]\n\nThe solutions to the equation are \\( x = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{41}}{2} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{41}}{2} \\). The sum of these solutions is:\n\\[ \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{41}}{2} + \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{41}}{2} = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{41} + 3 - \\sqrt{41}}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3. \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all \\( x \\) that satisfy the equation is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
