      "text": "From the definition of $f^{-1}(x)$, we know that $\\frac1{(2x)}=16x+3$. Solving for $x$, we get\n\\[x = \\frac{-1}{32}-\\frac{1}{32\\cdot 2^2}\\]\nThis means that there are two solutions, namely\n\\[x_1 = \\frac{-1}{32}, \\quad x_2 = \\frac{-35}{32}\\]\nThese two numbers are reciprocals of each other, so their sum is \\[\\boxed{0}\\] (of course, this result ignores any rational number solutions).\n\nSolution Explanation:\nWe notice the similarity in structure between the two functions on both sides of the equation. So, let's define a new function \n$$F(x) = f^{-1}(x)+\\frac{7}{32}.$$ We can see that\n$$F(f^{-1}(x))=F\\left(16x+3+\\frac{7}{32}\\right)=f(x)$$\nand\n$$F\\left(f(\\frac1{(2x)})\\right)=f^{-1}\\left(\\frac1{7x+3}+\\frac{7}{32}\\right)=\\frac1{(2x)}$$\nHence,\n$$F(f^{-1}(x))=F(f((2x)^{-1}))$$\nor\n$$f(f^{-1}(x))=f(\\frac1{(2x)})$$\nEquating the two expressions, we get\n$$f^{-1}(x) = \\frac1{(2x)},$$\nwhich can be solved easily to get the final answer as $\\boxed{0}$.",
