      "text": "Let $f^{-1}(x)=a$.\n$f(a)=f^{-1}(x)$, so $16a+3=f^{-1}(x)$. (1)\n$f((2x)^{-1})=a$, so $16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x}+3=a$ (2)\nEquation (1) simplifies to $16a+3=f^{-1}(x)$, and equation (2) simplifies to $\\frac{8}{x}+3=a$, so we can remove 8.5 from a. Here is why: $a=\\frac{16a}{2x}+\\frac{3}{2x}=\\frac{16a-8\\frac{}{x}}{2}+\\frac{3\\frac{}{x}}\n=\\frac{16\\frac{3}{x}}{2}$\nNow, we substitute 3.5 in the equation:\n$\\frac{8}{3.5}+3=\\frac{16a}{2x}+\\frac{3}{2x}$\n$\\frac{4}{7}+\\frac{21}{7}=\\frac{16a}{2x}+\\frac{3}{2x}$ (watch the 2s together)\n$\\frac{25}{7}=\\frac{16a}{2x}+\\frac{3}{2x}$\n$\\frac{25}{7}=\\frac{2a3/2\n...So answer is 27.5\nWow, that took forever. Just to take out 8/5. For anybody who spends a negative amount of time deciding whether to stick it out or not\nyou are wasting random people's time.\nI hate all these idiot-y solution-derive problem threads.\nDefinitely not maximizing their cases (\"Find the best scenario for this problem\"). \n\nDespite all the useless discussions, bonus topics, and general problems with people with inappropriate behavior, some problems can still be solved, though the solution sometimes takes a long time (for many better solvers).",
